  "reputation": {
    "summary": "The benchmark low-noise bipolar single, used in MC head amps (audioXpress MC100, Wyn Palmer), mic preamps and DAC I/V, and long set against the OPA627 on Head-Fi and diyAudio. Japanese reviewers reportedly hear a 'satin-finish', crisper sound. Soomal praises big dynamics and bass but found it too hard in the Creative AE-9.",
    "sonic_descriptors": [
      "sweet, sublime clarity",
      "satin-finish (Zigsow)",
      "robust, crisper than OPA627",
      "huge dynamics, powerful bass",
      "dry, emphasised bass/treble",
      "too hard (偏硬)"
    ],
    "typical_uses": [
      "Moving-coil phono head amps and RIAA preamps",
      "Microphone and console preamps",
      "DAC I/V converters",
      "Low-noise test and measurement front ends",
      "Single-channel rolling on a dual adapter"
    ],
    "caveats": [
      "Sensitive to layout, decoupling and capacitive load; not a safe blind drop-in (datasheet 'Driving Capacitive Loads').",
      "Single only; dual adapters add lead length and oscillation risk.",
      "About 2 pA/√Hz current noise: loses its edge with high source or feedback impedances. Works at gain 2.",
      "Draws about 8 mA per amplifier, more than typical audio duals.",
      "Heavily counterfeited, especially DIP parts (including ANZ) and any metal-can 'AD797'."
    ],
    "sources": [
